    "text": "To solve for the positive value of \\( t \\) such that \\( |6 + ti| = 10 \\), we need to understand the definition of the magnitude (or modulus) of a complex number. The magnitude of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nIn this problem, the complex number is \\( 6 + ti \\), so its magnitude is:\n\\[\n|6 + ti| = \\sqrt{6^2 + t^2}\n\\]\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to 10:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6^2 + t^2} = 10\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n6^2 + t^2 = 10^2\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n36 + t^2 = 100\n\\]\nNext, we isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 36 from both sides:\n\\[\nt^2 = 100 - 36\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 64\n\\]\nTo find \\( t \\), we take the positive square root of both sides (since we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\)):\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{64}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = 8\n\\]\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
